Advance training of physics	Normally when you run a DELETE statement in a database, the data’s gone.\n\nWith the soft delete design pattern, you add a bit column like IsDeleted, IsActive, or IsArchived to the table, and instead of deleting rows, you flip the bit column. This can buy you a few advantages:\n\nEasier/faster undeletes\nHistory tracking (keeping deleted rows around for auditing purposes, although the method I’m going to show here doesn’t do anything fancy like track who did the delete)\nEasier reconciliation during disaster recovery failovers (refer to the Senior DBA class module on recovering from failovers for more info)\nLower workload for Availability Groups secondaries (neat scenario from Aaron Bertrand)	# Introduction \n\nWelcome to this course, we are glad you made it here. It's going to be very interesting with you guys. Take care everyone\n\n# Calls\n\nOur lesson will be help via this google meet link\n\n## Conclusion\n\nEverything  you will need will be here	23823747-cba0-4768-a039-83c7eedc33c0	2021-08-12 10:47:20.953505+00	2023-10-13 00:00:00+00	10828276-77e2-4bb8-b7ea-38126ccbe348	t	https://tapaozmyjsuykgerrfkt.supabase.in/storage/v1/object/public/avatars/course/gravatar-template.png	\N	{}	0	NGN	\N	\N	f	\N	ACTIVE
