<h3>Activity 1: Identifying Objects</h3>\n\n<p>In this activity, students will practice identifying objects and their attributes in Python.</p>\n\n<p>1. Ask the students to think of common objects around them (e.g., a car, a book, a smartphone).</p>\n<p>2. Instruct them to identify the attributes of each object (e.g., a car may have attributes like color, model, and number of wheels).</p>\n<p>3. Now, ask the students to write Python code to define a class representing each object, with attributes that they identified.</p>\n<p>4. After they have defined the classes, encourage them to create instances of the classes and access the attributes of the objects.</p>\n<p>5. Finally, have the students share their code and discuss the different approaches and attributes they chose.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>Activity 2: Creating a Shape Hierarchy</h3>\n\n<p>In this activity, students will create a hierarchy of shapes using object-oriented programming in Python.</p>\n\n<p>1. Begin by discussing the concept of inheritance in object-oriented programming.</p>\n<p>2. Ask the students to think of different shapes (e.g., circle, square, triangle) and their common attributes (e.g., area, perimeter).</p>\n<p>3. Guide them in creating a base class called "Shape" with common attributes and methods for calculating area and perimeter.</p>\n<p>4. Instruct the students to create derived classes (subclasses) for each specific shape, inheriting from the base class "Shape" and adding any additional attributes or methods specific to that shape.</p>\n<p>5. Once they have defined the classes, encourage the students to create instances of different shapes and access their attributes and methods.</p>\n<p>6. Have the students compare and discuss the benefits of using inheritance to create the shape hierarchy.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>Activity 3: Simulating a Bank Account</h3>\n\n<p>In this activity, students will simulate a bank account using object-oriented programming in Python.</p>\n\n<p>1. Start by discussing the concept of a bank account and its attributes (e.g., account number, balance).</p>\n<p>2. Ask the students to define a class called "BankAccount" with attributes and methods to represent a bank account.</p>\n<p>3. Instruct them to include methods for depositing money, withdrawing money, and checking the account balance.</p>\n<p>4. Once they have defined the class, have the students create instances of the "BankAccount" class and perform various transactions (e.g., depositing money, withdrawing money).</p>\n<p>5. Encourage the students to handle cases where the account balance becomes negative and implement appropriate error handling.</p>\n<p>6. Finally, discuss with the students the benefits of using object-oriented programming for modeling real-world scenarios like a bank account.</p>	\N	\N	cf347003-3c83-45a4-8d80-1476422d38c4	d545593f-952d-42db-a43d-6b70ba40b7cc	2023-09-02 16:13:08.941508+00	2023-09-02 16:49:38.994033+00	Object Oriented Programming in Python	f	2023-09-02 16:12:51.272+00	0729a995-ce20-468d-aac3-f8d9ea927989	f	\N	4	f	[{"link": "https://pub-bd38ff7251aa402a93762181a947f7ea.r2.dev/lm29crp1lt8drgv39-y2mate.is -  3 Python Tutorial for Beginners Getting Started with Python-DWgzHbglNIo-480pp-1693673198.mp4", "type": "muse", "metadata": {"fid": "88e76f119cdcd055bcabaa361d6a791307e6aefc046e731cb161a7967713768f", "own": true, "url": "https://cdn.muse.ai/w/88e76f119cdcd055bcabaa361d6a791307e6aefc046e731cb161a7967713768f/data", "size": 27087867, "svid": "8M8SMKW", "title": "y2mate.is - 3 Python Tutorial for Beginners Getting Started with Python-DWgzHbglNIo-480pp-1693673198", "views": 0, "width": 854, "height": 480, "mature": false, "creator": true, "license": "owned", "duration": 888.81, "filename": "y2mate.is - 3 Python Tutorial for Beginners Getting Started with Python-DWgzHbglNIo-480pp-1693673198.mp4", "language": null, "tcreated": 1693673310, "twatched": 0, "ingesting": true, "visibility": "unlisted", "description": "", "ingest_video": 0}}]
